<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 682–720</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Umar II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umar ibn Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a>, known as Umar II, was the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 717 to 720. Remembered as the most pious of the dynasty, he sought to reform taxation so that non-Arab converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a>, were treated as equals of Arab Muslims, ended the cursing of Ali from the pulpit and withdrew the army from the failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. His short reign became the model of a just ruler in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-abd-al-aziz.jpg" alt="Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not credited · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Umar_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
